  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nA cold front will drop through the area from northwest to \nsoutheast early tomorrow. The 12Z runs of the moist-biased NAM, \nNAM Nest, and NSSL-WRF all try to spit out some rain in our area\nas the front and an associated shortwave roll through tomorrow \nmorning. However, the lack of more appreciable mid-upper-level \ncloud cover or any real signs of virga over North Dakota at this\nhour suggest that these models are overestimating the strength \nof this shortwave and the amount of moisture associated with it.\nThe model guidance that has a better handle of what's currently\ngoing on over North Dakota maintains precipitation-free \nconditions along the front as it tracks through our forecast \narea, so a dry frontal passage is still expected tomorrow. While\nmodest cold air advection will occur off of northwesterly winds\nbehind the front, relatively deep post-frontal mixing and \nmostly sunny skies should nevertheless support high temperatures\ntomorrow that will be near or slightly higher than today's \nhighs in the mid 70s to low 80s (warmest in our southern \ncounties).\n\nCooler high temperatures in the mid 60s to mid 70s are \nultimately expected on Thursday amidst dry conditions as modest \nnorthwesterly to northerly winds persist and the heart of the \ncooler air mass and an attendant surface high settle into the \nregion. Similar to slightly warmer temperature readings are then\nfavored again during the daytime on Friday as southerly return \nflow becomes established over the region. There remains a signal\nin ensemble guidance for warm air advection/isentropic ascent \nto induce some showers or sprinkles in the region on Friday as \nmid-level lapse rates steepen modestly. However, there is still \nnot much consistency with where this precipitation may occur, \nand dry low-mid air is also a factor that precludes higher \nconfidence in the occurrence of measurable rainfall here on \nFriday. The latest NBM's mentionable PoPs remain displaced just\nto our north and northwest, and this still looks reasonable for\nnow.\n\nPersisting warm air advection off of southerly winds will lead \nto warmer temperatures on Saturday, with low-mid 80s high \ntemperatures looking likely across a good chunk of our forecast \narea amidst sunny or mostly sunny skies. The forecast for Sunday\nremains more up in the air, in large part due to considerable \nuncertainties over how a tropical disturbance (invest 92L) in \nthe Gulf will evolve in the coming days. Broadly speaking, this \ndisturbance is expected to become an organized tropical system \nbefore making landfall along the Gulf Coast and encountering a \nbroad upper-level trough. Its remnants and the associated \ntropical moisture will initially surge northward before turning \nnortheastward towards the Mid-Atlantic/New England. How quickly \nthis northeastward turn occurs will dictate whether any \nassociated rain gets into our forecast area, which, in turn, \nwill also have an influence on temperatures for Sunday. While \nmost Grand Ensemble members still favor this activity remaining \nto our southeast, ensemble support for a solution where rain \ngets into at least our southeastern counties has steadily \nincreased over the past few model cycles. The latest NBM came in\nwith chance PoPs across our southeastern CWA as a result, but \nif this northwestward trend continues to garner more support, \nthen these PoPs may need to be increased and/or expanded \nfurther.\n\nOgorek",
